          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Ground-based telescopes face a stubborn problem: turbulence in Earth's atmosphere blurs the light arriving from distant stars, smearing the fine detail astronomers most want to study. Astrophysicist Andrea Ghez of the University of California, Los Angeles, helped change what such telescopes could see by championing the use of adaptive optics, a technique in which a deformable mirror reshapes itself many times each second to cancel out the atmospheric distortions in real time. Working with the Keck Observatory in Hawaii, Ghez and her collaborators showed that adaptive optics could deliver images of the crowded region around the galactic center sharper than those previously obtainable from space-based instruments. By demonstrating that the technique could meet the demands of cutting-edge research rather than serving only as a specialty tool, Ghez's work made adaptive optics a routine part of how astronomers now study faint, closely spaced objects.",
