          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When astronomers ask whether a planet beyond our solar system could harbor life, they often reach for a list of so-called biosignature gases — molecules whose presence in a planet's atmosphere would be hard to explain without biological activity. Planetary scientist Sara Seager of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology has argued that this list has been drawn too narrowly. Most prior catalogs focused on a small handful of gases, especially oxygen and methane, that are produced by familiar Earth life. Seager and her collaborators surveyed the chemistry of thousands of small molecules and proposed that on planets with atmospheres very different from Earth's — hydrogen-rich, for instance, or hot and rocky — many other compounds could plausibly serve as biosignatures, while some of the standard Earth-based candidates would lose their diagnostic value. Her argument reframes the search by tying the menu of useful biosignature gases to the specific atmospheric conditions of each planet rather than to a universal Earth-derived shortlist.",
