          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, who spent much of his career at the University of Chicago, was known for moving systematically through one branch of theoretical astrophysics at a time, publishing a comprehensive monograph on each branch before turning to the next. One such cycle, completed in the 1950s, focused on radiative transfer — the mathematics of how light propagates through a medium that absorbs, emits, and scatters it. Earlier treatments of the problem had relied on a patchwork of approximate methods designed for specific physical settings, such as planetary atmospheres or stellar interiors. Chandrasekhar instead developed a unified set of equations and solution techniques that applied across these settings and that other researchers could adapt to new problems without rebuilding the formalism each time. His work on radiative transfer is now treated as a foundational reference precisely because it consolidated previously scattered methods into a single coherent framework.",
