          "passage": "In My Face Is Black Is True (2005), the historian Mary Frances Berry of the University of Pennsylvania reconstructs the late-nineteenth-century pension movement led by Callie House, a former laundress who organized hundreds of thousands of formerly enslaved people to petition the federal government for compensation. Berry shows that the movement built mass dues-paying chapters across the South and developed a national correspondence network. She also documents that federal officials pursued House for years on charges of mail fraud and that internal Post Office memoranda explicitly framed her organization as dangerous because it threatened to make reparations a popular political demand. Berry's account therefore implies that the federal response to House's organization ______",
