          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises about Feimster's research: (1) earlier accounts treated lynching as a story driven mainly by men and assigned women only symbolic roles, and (2) Feimster documents that Black women organized sustained anti-lynching campaigns and that some white women publicly defended lynch mobs in print and in political organizations. The minimum inference these premises jointly force is that an analysis attending only to male protagonists will overlook a substantial part of the political activity organizing and contesting lynching — because by Feimster's evidence, women on both sides were operating as political actors, not as the symbolic figures earlier accounts described. Choice A captures exactly this measured claim and stays within the evidence the passage actually provides, hedging with 'a substantial part' rather than 'the entirety' of the political activity.\n\nThis is a sufficient-vs-necessary distinction. The premises establish that attending to women's political organizing is necessary to a complete account of the period; they do not establish that women's organizing was sufficient on its own to explain the period or that men's roles were unimportant. The correct answer must respect that asymmetry.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D (scope shift / sufficient-vs-necessary trap): Sounds aligned with Feimster's emphasis on women's political activity and uses dramatic language ('cannot reach any defensible historical conclusions') that students may associate with a strong revisionist thesis. But the premises establish only that women's political activity was substantial and overlooked, not that lynching was driven 'entirely' by women's organizing. The passage is consistent with the view that men's roles also mattered; it argues that women's roles also did. Choice D inverts a necessity claim into a sufficiency claim.\n- Choice C (inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). Feimster's documented evidence of organized women's political activity on both sides is precisely what undercuts the earlier accounts that limited women to symbolic roles, so the premises license the opposite conclusion from the one C states.\n- Choice B (inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage names sustained anti-lynching campaigns and public political defenses of lynch mobs as activities Feimster documents; these are political traces, so the claim that the women 'left no measurable trace' on political conflicts reverses what the evidence shows.",
