          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In Coming of Age in Samoa (1928), the anthropologist Margaret Mead reported on adolescents she had observed in a Samoan village and pressed a comparative argument that ran against widely held assumptions in the United States. American writers of the period commonly treated the storms of adolescence — emotional volatility, rebelliousness, sharp generational conflict — as a biological inevitability of the teenage years. Mead's claim, drawn from her fieldwork, was that this turbulence was not a universal feature of human development but a particular cultural pattern; the Samoan adolescents she described moved through the same physical transition without the same emotional crisis, suggesting that what looked inherent to the life stage was in fact shaped by the surrounding social arrangements.",
