          "passage": "In Mohawk Interruptus: Political Life Across the Borders of Settler States (2014), the Columbia anthropologist Audra Simpson, herself a member of the Mohawk community of Kahnawake, develops what she calls a politics of refusal. The standard analytic move, she observes, is to treat the absence of Canadian or U.S. citizenship registration in Kahnawake as a passive gap left behind by withdrawal — a community that opted out of state political life. Simpson presses an opposite reading: refusal, on her account, is not the negative space left when a population steps back from the settler state but a continuing, substantive practice of Mohawk nationhood, in which councils, decisions, and territorial boundaries are exercised as the operative authority in everyday life.",
