          "explanation": "The correct answer is D, 'who.' The blank introduces a relative clause modifying the plural noun 'scientists,' and within that clause the relative pronoun functions as the subject of the verb 'rely.' Standard English uses the subjective form 'who' when the relative pronoun is the subject of its own clause, so 'Project scientists who rely on her pointing-stability budgets' is the grammatically correct construction. The pronoun's case is set by its role inside the relative clause, not by the surface position of the noun it modifies.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C ('whose'): surface match — 'whose' belongs to the same who-family of relative pronouns and visually resembles the correct answer, but it is the possessive form. Substituting it produces 'scientists whose rely on her pointing-stability budgets,' which forces 'rely' to be read as a possessed noun and yields an ungrammatical clause with no main verb.\n- Choice A ('whom'): inverse — 'whom' is the objective form of the same pronoun and would be correct only if the relative pronoun functioned as an object inside the clause (as in 'scientists whom Ericsson advises'). Here the pronoun is the subject of 'rely,' so the objective case is the wrong form.\n- Choice B ('which'): scope-shift — a different SEC error: switching pronoun type rather than pronoun case. 'Which' is a relative pronoun reserved for non-persons in Standard English, so applying it to the human antecedent 'scientists' violates the person-vs-nonperson distinction rather than the subjective-vs-objective case distinction the item tests.",
