          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The first sentence describes Anning's work at Lyme Regis: she recovered marine-reptile skeletons that challenged prevailing ideas about the antiquity and diversity of those animals. The second sentence describes a parallel project by a contemporary, Mantell, who was assembling terrestrial-reptile remains that pointed toward an unfamiliar new group. The two sentences set up two researchers, working in different beds and on different animals, who were independently doing the same kind of work — recovering fossils that disturbed the period's settled categories. The relation is parallel continuation: A did X; in the same period and on the same line of thought, B did the analogous Y. 'Likewise,' is the CB-lexicon transition for that 'and in the same vein' move, and is correct (A).\n\nThe single-word cue that selects 'Likewise,' here is 'contemporary': it frames Mantell as a peer doing parallel work, which is the textbook signature of continuation rather than contrast or consequence.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (B) 'However,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals contrast or reversal: it would tell the reader that Mantell's work cut against Anning's. Sentence 2 does the opposite — it pairs Mantell's terrestrial-reptile assembly with Anning's marine-reptile recovery as the same kind of disturbance to received categories — so 'However,' points in exactly the wrong direction.\n- (D) 'Therefore,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals causal consequence: because Anning recovered her specimens, Mantell pursued his work. There is no such causal claim in either sentence; the two researchers worked independently in different beds, and sentence 2 does not present Mantell's project as a downstream effect of Anning's discoveries.\n- (C) 'For instance,' (scope shift — wrong relation) signals that sentence 2 illustrates the general claim of sentence 1 with a specific case. But Mantell is not an instance of Anning's marine-reptile work — he is a parallel investigator on different animals in different beds. 'For instance,' would fit if sentence 2 named one of Anning's specimens; instead, it introduces a separate researcher doing analogous work.",
