          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The first two sentences establish a then-frame. Sentence 1 anchors the field's earlier consensus in the 1950s — the carved dates were treated as exclusively ritual, on the assumption that Maya inscriptions did not record named historical actors. Sentence 2 anchors Proskouriakoff's 1960 intervention: the statistical intervals between dates matched human lifespans too closely to be ritual and were better read as birth and accession years of named rulers. Sentence 3 then shifts to the present tense — 'historians . . . treat the figures . . . as kings and queens whose reigns can be dated to the year' — to describe the current standard reading of the monuments and the surrounding glyphs. The relation between the prior sentences and sentence 3 is a then-versus-now contrast: a 1960 reinterpretation has, in the intervening decades, become the field's settled framework. 'Today,' is the CB-lexicon transition for that historical-to-present move and is correct (D).\n\nThe single-word cue that selects 'Today,' here is 'Through the 1950s' / '1960' in sentences 1-2, paired with the present-tense 'treat' and 'can be dated' in sentence 3: together they signal a temporal shift from a specific past era to the current state of the field, which is exactly what 'Today,' marks.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (C) 'Previously,' (inverse — opposite logical move) reverses the temporal arrow: it would frame sentence 3 as describing the earlier situation and the first two sentences as describing the later one. The first two sentences, however, are the ones explicitly anchored in the 1950s and 1960, and sentence 3 uses the present tense to describe current scholarly practice. 'Previously,' points in exactly the wrong direction.\n- (A) 'Moreover,' (scope shift — too weak for the relation) signals that sentence 3 contributes a further point on the same line of thought as sentence 2. That is too flat: sentence 3 does not add another argument from Proskouriakoff's 1960 paper; it shifts the time frame to the present and describes what her reinterpretation has become in current practice. 'Moreover,' would fit if sentence 3 introduced a separate fact from Proskouriakoff's own work; instead, it marks a temporal transition from then to now.\n- (B) 'Nevertheless,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals concession: sentence 3 holds despite something in the prior sentences. The reading is plausible because sentence 1 names a contrary consensus, but sentence 2 already does the work of overturning that consensus on Proskouriakoff's evidence. Sentence 3 is not a third position holding against complications in sentence 2; it reports the present-day acceptance of sentence 2's reinterpretation. 'Nevertheless,' would be the right move if it sat between sentences 1 and 2; it points in the wrong direction at the boundary between sentences 2 and 3.",
