          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"explained.\" The passage frames Hahn's barium signature as an unexpected finding that most physicists were dismissing as contamination. Meitner's response, by contrast, was to give a physical account of the result: she treats the uranium nucleus as a charged liquid drop, traces what the incoming neutron does to that drop, and uses the mass difference between parent and products to account for the released energy. The verb in the blank therefore needs to mean to give a reasoned account of why the surprising result occurred. \"Explained\" matches that meaning precisely; the rest of the sentence then sets out the explanation she actually offered.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D: surface match — \"described\" is the verb most surface-similar to the passage's reportorial tone, since the sentence does describe what Meitner wrote in her correspondence. But to describe a finding is merely to state what was observed, and the passage credits Meitner with more than that: she does not just restate Hahn's barium result, she gives a physical mechanism (the liquid-drop division) that accounts for it. The verb has to capture the reasoning, not just the reporting.\n- Choice C: scope shift — \"memorized\" picks up the academic, study-oriented register of the surrounding text and would be a familiar verb for a student to associate with a scientist working from notes. But to memorize is to commit something to memory, which is a wrong kind of action for the relationship the sentence describes. Meitner is not retaining Hahn's result; she is producing an interpretation of it.\n- Choice B: inverse — \"concealed\" reverses the direction the passage establishes. The sentence presents Meitner as the colleague who, alone among most of her contemporaries, takes the barium signature seriously and works out what it means; concealing the finding would describe the opposite stance, in which the result is hidden rather than interpreted.",
