          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Sui Sin Far's 1912 short story \"Mrs. Spring Fragrance.\" Mrs. Spring Fragrance, who has been in America for five years, has just returned from a lecture and is reporting on it to her American neighbor, Mrs. Carman.\n\n\"The lecture was most beautiful,\" said Mrs. Spring Fragrance. \"The speaker, who was an American gentleman, said that when a man and a woman in this country love one another, they are as the fingers of one hand, that the joy of one is the joy of the other, and the trouble of one the trouble of the other. He said also that an American husband would do anything for the wife of his heart, and an American wife would yield up her every wish to the man whom she calls her own.\"\n\nMrs. Carman smiled but said nothing.\n\n\"Now in China,\" continued Mrs. Spring Fragrance, \"it is not always so. There a man's mother may decide for him whom he shall marry, and the wife of his heart, should he have one, must walk in the path which has been marked out for them both before they were born. I think the American way is more beautiful — and yet, when I look at you and at my own husband, I wonder whether it is so different after all, or whether the difference lies only in the words we use to describe it.\"",
