            {
              "label": "Text 1",
              "text": "In Shakespearean Negotiations (1988), the literary critic Stephen Greenblatt set out to read Shakespeare's plays as participants in the broader circulation of social energy in Elizabethan England — the practices, idioms, and authorities by which power organized itself in the period. The plays, on his account, do not stand outside that circulation as detached commentary; they take up the energies of court, church, and market, redistribute them across their own scenes, and return them to the audience reshaped. Greenblatt was particularly interested in moments where the staged action seemed to license a subversive possibility — a deposed monarch, a witty servant outwitting an aristocrat, a daughter resisting paternal authority — and his characteristic argument was that such moments rarely escape the system that produces them. The plays, by appearing to release subversive energies under the contained conditions of performance, often did the work of containment itself: they let the audience entertain alternatives whose theatrical airing left the social order it implicitly questioned more, rather than less, secure. The Shakespearean stage, in his account, is therefore most fully understood as a site at which subversion is licensed precisely so it can be safely absorbed."
            },
            {
              "label": "Text 2",
              "text": "The Renaissance scholar Catherine Belsey, in The Subject of Tragedy (1985) and subsequent essays, takes the same historicist starting point Greenblatt does: the plays of the period are best read against the cultural and political grammar of early modern England, and they participate in shaping the subject that grammar produces. Where she parts company is on what status to give the moments Greenblatt identifies as containment. Reading the period drama, Shakespeare's included, Belsey finds the same scenes Greenblatt cites — a daughter's refusal, a wife's silence, a player's metatheatrical aside — but reads them as fissures in early modern subjectivity that the closing acts cannot smoothly resolve, especially when the speakers are women, servants, or others to whom early modern discourse offered no stable position. The closure Greenblatt's argument requires of the plays does not, in her view, reliably arrive. Containment, she allows, is one possible outcome of staged subversion. What it is not, on her reading, is the master pattern; the plays often leave their fissures uncovered, available to be read by audiences then and now as evidence that the early modern self was less settled than the period's official self-descriptions implied."
            }
