          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Text 2 is explicit about the methodological ground Belsey shares with Greenblatt: \"she takes the same historicist starting point Greenblatt does,\" treating the plays as best read against the cultural and political grammar of early modern England and as participants in shaping the subject that grammar produces. Her quarrel is not with the historicist starting point but with the status Greenblatt assigns to the scenes he reads as containment. She finds \"the same scenes Greenblatt cites — a daughter's refusal, a wife's silence, a player's metatheatrical aside\" — and reads them as \"fissures in early modern subjectivity that the closing acts cannot smoothly resolve, especially when the speakers are women, servants, or others to whom early modern discourse offered no stable position.\" Her load-bearing claim is the elevation question: containment, she allows, is \"one possible outcome of staged subversion\"; \"what it is not . . . is the master pattern.\" Choice D captures exactly this combination — historicism preserved, containment refused as the master pattern, fissures in early modern subjectivity (with the same exemplars Belsey names) presented as evidence the closing acts do not smoothly resolve.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — A casts Belsey as repudiating historicism altogether and reading Shakespeare as autonomous aesthetic objects. Text 2 says the opposite in its first sentence: she takes \"the same historicist starting point\" Greenblatt does. A confuses her resistance to one of historicism's typical conclusions (containment as master pattern) with a refusal of the methodological frame they actually share.\n- Choice C: inverse — C reverses Belsey's stance by treating containment as correct for the major tragedies and explaining the counterexamples away as later collaborators' work. Text 2 explicitly denies that containment is the master pattern and grounds the counterexamples (resisting daughter, silent wife, metatheatrical aside) in the plays themselves, not in posthumous interventions. The choice flips Belsey from a critic of containment-as-master-pattern into one of its defenders.\n- Choice B: scope shift — D imports a methodological controversy (neuroscientific accounts of audience response) that has no anchor in either text. Belsey's disagreement with Greenblatt is internal to historicist literary scholarship; she contests the elevation of containment from within shared methodological commitments, not from a different discipline. The scope shift turns a precise intramural quarrel into a cross-disciplinary supersession.",
