          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Astrophysicist Wanda Diaz Merced, who works with the International Astronomical Union's Office of Astronomy for Development, has used her own training to argue that the design of scientific tools shapes who can become a scientist. After Diaz Merced lost her sight as a graduate student, conventional plot-based software effectively closed off the kinds of time-series analysis her field expected of her, and she shifted to studying space-physics data through sound. In a series of talks and policy papers, she has reported that this shift was made possible only by colleagues willing to share unprocessed data and by access to programmable analysis environments she could adapt herself. From these experiences Diaz Merced argues that broadening participation in astronomy is less a matter of separate accommodations than of building data-analysis pipelines and training programs that assume from the outset that researchers will work with information through more than one sensory channel.",
