          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When Mae Jemison left NASA in 1993, the year after her flight aboard the STS-47 Spacelab-J mission, she did not move into another single research role but instead launched several initiatives at once. She founded a technology consulting firm aimed at helping under-resourced communities adopt new tools, and in 1994 she opened an international science camp for teenagers that asked participants to tackle real engineering problems alongside professional scientists. In the years that followed, she continued to write about why long-horizon scientific programs — efforts that span decades rather than single missions — depend on educational pipelines and on social institutions that can sustain interest across generations. Across these projects, Jemison consistently treats access to advanced technology, hands-on training for young people, and the cultivation of long-running institutions as parts of a single strategy rather than as separate causes.",
