          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "By the late 1980s, computer simulations of Earth's general circulation had grown detailed enough that some commentators described them as miniature weather forecasters that, given the right inputs, could predict regional climate. In a 1990 study, atmospheric scientist Warren Washington and a colleague at the National Center for Atmospheric Research used one of these models to test that framing. They imposed a sustained warm sea-surface temperature anomaly in the tropical Pacific and tracked the simulated atmospheric response across the globe. The model reproduced robust large-scale features — broad shifts in tropical rainfall belts and remote changes in the wintertime circulation over North America — but Washington was careful to argue that those large-scale signals did not license confident statements about specific regional outcomes, which the same simulations rendered far less consistently. The study's lesson, in his telling, was less that the model could forecast regional climate than that it could establish which large-scale teleconnections a given forcing should produce while leaving regional detail beyond its reliable reach.",
          "question": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?",
