          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises about Perry's argument: (1) arrangements first worked out in the South — labor organization, citizenship, racial hierarchy — were absorbed into national institutions and continue to organize life across the country, and (2) conflicts and crises commonly described as national in scope follow patterns rehearsed in Southern settings before they became national. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that a study which sets the South aside is likely to misread features of national life whose institutional shape and continuing logic were Southern in origin. Choice B states exactly this measured claim and uses 'is likely to misread' to capture the implication without overstating it.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D (inverse): Directly contradicts both premises. The passage states that Southern arrangements were absorbed into national institutions and that national-scope conflicts follow patterns rehearsed in Southern settings, which is the opposite of 'largely separate institutional paths.'\n- Choice A (scope shift / sufficiency trap): Pushes the necessity claim — that the South must be attended to for national analysis to be accurate — into a sufficiency claim that the South 'alone determines every important political and economic outcome.' The premises license the conclusion that ignoring the South leads to misreading; they do not license the stronger view that the South single-handedly fixes the national outcome.\n- Choice C (scope shift): Imports a temporal distinction — exceptional in the nineteenth century, integrated thereafter — that the passage does not make. Perry's account treats the absorption of Southern arrangements into national institutions as continuing to organize present-day life, so the premises do not support the proposed periodization.",
