          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In America for Americans (2019), the historian Erika Lee of Harvard traces the history of xenophobia in the United States from the early republic to the present. Lee documents two recurring features of that history. First, exclusion laws, naturalization restrictions, and deportation campaigns directed at successive immigrant groups were typically enacted through ordinary congressional procedure and routinely upheld by federal courts, indicating that xenophobic policies were embedded in standard institutional channels rather than imposed through emergency or extraordinary means. Second, the same pattern of restriction reappears, with different targets, in nearly every generation that her account surveys. Lee therefore argues against the view that American xenophobia represents an occasional departure from a tolerant national norm. Her two observations together imply that any account of immigration policy that treats restrictionist episodes as exceptional lapses from a tolerant baseline ______",
