          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In From the War on Poverty to the War on Crime (2016), the historian Elizabeth Hinton of Yale reconstructs how federal policy on urban inequality and federal policy on crime control developed during the second half of the twentieth century. Hinton draws on previously underused archival material to document two findings. First, mid-1960s Great Society planning bodies — staffed by many of the same officials who designed federal anti-poverty programs — also drafted and circulated the early federal templates for urban crime-control surveillance, policing, and detention. Second, those same templates were carried directly into the more punitive federal legislation of the late 1960s and 1970s with relatively little institutional discontinuity. Hinton's account therefore implies that any explanation of late-twentieth-century mass incarceration that treats it as a sharp punitive break with the Great Society's anti-poverty agenda ______",
