          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises from Hinton's archival work: (1) mid-1960s Great Society planning bodies — staffed by many of the same officials who designed federal anti-poverty programs — also drafted the early federal templates for urban crime-control surveillance, policing, and detention, and (2) those templates were carried directly into the punitive federal legislation of the late 1960s and 1970s with relatively little institutional discontinuity. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that an explanation treating late-twentieth-century mass incarceration as a sharp punitive break with the Great Society's anti-poverty agenda leaves out an institutional connection that actually links the two periods — the same personnel and templates ran continuously between them. Choice B states exactly that measured claim and stays within the institutional history the premises establish.\n\nThis is a sufficient-vs-necessary distinction. The premises license a continuity claim about personnel and templates; they do not license claims about the original architects' explicit intent to produce mass incarceration, nor do they require that no other developments influenced the punitive turn. The correct answer must respect what the institutional evidence actually establishes.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C (inverse): Directly contradicts both premises. The passage states that mid-1960s planning bodies were staffed by many of the same officials who designed anti-poverty programs and that crime-control templates were carried into later punitive legislation with little institutional discontinuity, which is the opposite of 'institutionally distinct.'\n- Choice A (scope shift / sufficiency trap): Pushes a claim about institutional continuity into a claim about explicit intent. The premises establish that the personnel and templates were continuous; they do not establish that the Great Society architects 'explicitly intended' their planning to culminate in mass incarceration, which is a much stronger psychological claim the evidence does not address.\n- Choice D (inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the punitive legislation of the late 1960s and 1970s carried forward templates from earlier Great Society planning, so the conditional D presents — treating the legislation as having relied on entirely new statutory frameworks — describes a counterfactual world Hinton's evidence rules out, not the world the premises establish.",
