          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In The Boundaries of Blackness: AIDS and the Breakdown of Black Politics (1999), the political scientist Cathy J. Cohen, of the University of Chicago, examines why mainstream Black political organizations in the United States were slow to mobilize against the AIDS epidemic during its first decade. The standard explanation pointed outward, to the broader social stigma surrounding the disease. Cohen develops a different account, which she calls secondary marginalization: the slow response, on her view, was not merely a product of external prejudice but a predictable internal pattern by which an already-marginalized group, in trying to defend its broader political standing, contracts the boundaries of who its politics will publicly defend, pushing already-stigmatized members — particularly gay men, intravenous drug users, and trans women — to the edge of the constituency.",
