          "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The claim has two specific parts: (1) the slow response is not merely a product of external prejudice but an internal pattern, and (2) that pattern is one in which an already-marginalized group, in trying to defend its broader standing, contracts the boundaries of who its politics will publicly defend — pushing the most-stigmatized of its own members to the edge. Choice C maps onto both: it names the internal mechanism — \"an already-marginalized community, anxious to protect its standing, redraws its political map\" — and identifies who is pushed out as a result — \"some of its own — those carrying the heaviest stigma — fall outside the lines its leaders are willing to defend in public.\" That is a direct, scoped match to the secondary-marginalization claim the passage attributes to Cohen.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice D: surface-match — the quotation lives in the same general territory of the AIDS epidemic and uses the recognizable register of political-history commentary. But its content concerns the federal response and partisan calculations, not the internal contracting of constituency boundaries within Black political organizations. It does not speak to the secondary-marginalization mechanism the underlined claim spotlights.\n- Choice B: scope-shift — the quotation engages with the institutional inheritance of church-affiliated networks, a topic adjacent to Cohen's project, but it makes a point about meeting-calendar structures rather than about the boundary-drawing pattern that produces the slow response. It addresses a different sub-claim of the book and does not illustrate the internal-pattern argument the passage foregrounds.\n- Choice A: inverse — the quotation contradicts the spirit of the claim. It locates the slow response \"entirely from outside the community,\" attributing it to an external public failure rather than to the internal contracting of group boundaries; choosing it would have Cohen endorsing the very external-stigma-only account the passage says her concept of secondary marginalization is meant to displace.",
