          "passage": "In a series of spectroscopic measurements at the Carnegie Institution of Washington in the late 1960s and early 1970s, the astronomer Vera Rubin and her collaborator Kent Ford predicted that if the visible mass of a spiral galaxy accounted for the gravitational pull on its stars, then rotational velocities should fall off steadily at large radii from the galactic center, just as the orbital speeds of planets fall off with distance from the Sun. ______ when Rubin and Ford plotted the rotation curves they had recorded for dozens of spiral galaxies of different sizes and morphologies, the velocities at large radii did not fall off at all but stayed nearly flat — exactly the signature their visible-mass prediction had said would not appear, and the same flat profile recurred across galaxy after galaxy.",
