          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The first sentence states a prediction Rubin and Ford derived from a visible-mass model of spiral galaxies: rotational velocities at large radii should fall off steadily, on the analogy of planets in the solar system. The second sentence reports what their measurements actually showed — and the language is pointed: the velocities at large radii 'did not fall off at all but stayed nearly flat,' which is 'exactly the signature their visible-mass prediction had said would not appear,' and the same flat profile recurred across many galaxies. The relation between the two sentences is reversal of expectation: the data overturned the very prediction sentence 1 set up. 'However,' is the standard CB-lexicon transition for that 'but the evidence pointed the other way' move, and is correct (D).\n\nThe single-word cue that selects 'However,' here is 'did not . . . at all' (reinforced by 'exactly the signature their . . . prediction had said would not appear'): both signal that sentence 2 contradicts the prediction in sentence 1, rather than continuing it, following from it, or merely intensifying it.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (C) 'As a result,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals causal consequence: because Rubin and Ford derived a prediction, they then made the measurements. There is a loose historical truth to that reading — the prediction did motivate the spectroscopic program — but the second sentence does not describe the measurements happening as a downstream effect of the prediction; it describes what those measurements observed, and the observed values contradict the prediction. The relation between the sentences is reversal of a claim, not consequence of an action.\n- (B) 'Likewise,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals continuation or parallel agreement: it would tell the reader that the rotation-curve data ran on the same line as the visible-mass prediction. Sentence 2 says the opposite — the velocities did not fall off as predicted but stayed nearly flat — so 'Likewise,' points in exactly the wrong direction.\n- (A) 'In addition,' (scope shift — wrong relation) signals that sentence 2 adds another point on the same line of thought as sentence 1. Topically, both sentences are about Rubin and Ford's work on spiral galaxies, so the choice can feel like a smooth continuation — but the second sentence does not contribute a separate, additional finding alongside the prediction; it overturns the prediction on the prediction's own terms, which is contrast, not addition.",
